---
import Feature from '../../components/Feature.astro'
import Snapshot from '../../components/Snapshot.astro'
import Specimen from '../../components/Specimen.astro'
import { FEATURES } from '../../features'
import FeaturePage from '../../layouts/FeaturePage.astro'
import type { PageMeta } from '../../meta'

export const meta = {
  description: FEATURES['desktop-and-terminal'].sentence,
  sources: [
    'docs/tui.md',
    'docs/tui/process.md',
    'docs/tui/plugin-losses.md',
    'docs/ui-design/shell-hierarchy.md',
    'docs/ui-design/closed-kit.md',
    'docs/panes/layout.md',
    'docs/cli.md',
    'docs/first-party-plugins/terminal.md',
  ],
  checked: '663359ff7',
} satisfies PageMeta
---

<FeaturePage feature="desktop-and-terminal" meta={meta} related={['plugins', 'nodes', 'automation']} docs={{ href: '/docs/start/desktop-and-terminal/', label: 'Read about the two clients' }}>
  <Snapshot slot="picture" id="terminal-small" />

  <Feature title="Two clients of one Node" href="/docs/start/desktop-and-terminal/" link="Desktop and terminal">
    <p>
      Run <code>acorn</code> with no arguments and the terminal client opens the workspace on this machine's Node. It
      attaches to the Node that's running, or starts one, so the desktop app and the terminal client can be open on
      the same tasks and agent sessions at once. The desktop app runs on macOS and Windows. The terminal client runs
      wherever Node.js 22, 24, or 26 does, Linux included.
    </p>
    <Snapshot slot="picture" id="hero-terminal" />
  </Feature>

  <Feature title="The same panes, from the same code" href="/docs/components/one-kit-two-hosts/" link="One kit, two hosts">
    <p>
      A pane is a tree of acorn's own components, and each client draws that tree: the desktop in HTML, the terminal
      in character cells. No plugin writes terminal UI or knows which client it's in. The command palette and the
      keyboard commands are shared too.
    </p>
    <Specimen slot="picture" id="issues-pane" both />
  </Feature>

  <Feature title="What the terminal draws its own way" href="/docs/start/desktop-and-terminal/" link="Desktop and terminal">
    <p>
      The terminal shows one pane at a time, with a strip of pane labels above it, and three panels on the left: the
      sources, what's under the chosen one, and the tasks. A graph that the desktop draws as cards and curves becomes
      an indented list in the same order. A plugin that draws its own web page gets one line that points at the
      desktop app. A plugin's <code>Cmd</code> shortcut is pressed with <code>Ctrl</code>.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature title="The command line, for scripts" href="/docs/automation/the-command-line/" link="The command line">
    <p>
      With a subcommand, <code>acorn</code> is a command-line interface (CLI) to a Node: list and create tasks, start
      and wait on agent sessions and workflow runs, and call a plugin's own commands. Every command prints a table,
      JSON, or JSON Lines, and exits with a code that says what went wrong. The desktop app can install the command
      for you, from <strong>Settings → Command line</strong>.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Fragment slot="terminal">
    <p>
      Almost every pane crosses unchanged. The editor's pane can run your own <code>$EDITOR</code> in place of its
      built-in editor. The Preview pane and the terminal drawer aren't there, though a terminal still draws
      wherever a pane opens one. The Agent pane can't attach or save files, and its settings pages need the desktop
      app. <a href="/docs/built-in-plugins/">Built-in plugins</a>
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</FeaturePage>
